1
00:00:06,633 --> 00:00:14,066
Why standardize? First, features should have similar sizes; else one in the thousands dominates one near one.

2
00:00:14,066 --> 00:00:20,400
Second, there are numerical issues: mixing huge and tiny values wastes floating-point precision.

3
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:27,833
Take a feature that runs from 100 to 200, plus an intercept column. The two columns nearly point the same way.

4
00:00:27,833 --> 00:00:36,333
The condition number is about six hundred thousand. Subtract the mean, divide by the deviation: exactly one.

5
00:00:36,333 --> 00:00:45,300
A ReLU unit bends at x equal to minus b over w. With standard normal w and b, that kink is a Cauchy variable.

6
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:52,466
Its variance is infinite, yet half its mass lies within one of zero, and ninety percent within seven.

7
00:00:52,466 --> 00:01:00,033
Now suppose the data live between 100 and 200. A kink lands there with chance about 0.16 percent per unit.

8
00:01:00,033 --> 00:01:07,566
Among a thousand hidden units, only one or two bend inside the data. The rest are zero or straight lines.

9
00:01:07,566 --> 00:01:16,033
Non-standardized data thus wastes the nonlinearity's expressive power. The same holds for tanh and sigmoid.

10
00:01:16,033 --> 00:01:23,733
A unit with no kink in the data is zero or a straight line there. All thousand stack into a few rank-one pieces.

11
00:01:23,733 --> 00:01:31,566
Take 50 data points. The raw feature matrix has only three singular values above zero; standardized, all fifty.

12
00:01:31,566 --> 00:01:38,766
That gives a cheap debugging check: when a model seems weak, look at the rank of its features.

13
00:01:38,766 --> 00:01:46,566
How to initialize? All zeros fails: everything is multiplied by zero, gradients vanish, nothing moves.

14
00:01:46,566 --> 00:01:52,900
Key observation: one layer's outputs feed the next, so every layer's inputs should look standardized.

15
00:01:52,900 --> 00:02:00,333
For d independent, zero-mean, unit-variance inputs, the output variance is d times the weight variance.

16
00:02:00,333 --> 00:02:09,533
Choose weight variance one over d, the fan-in, and the output is unit-variance too: Xavier initialization.

17
00:02:09,533 --> 00:02:17,400
Push standardized inputs through ten ReLU layers of width 256, with three weight choices, tracking size.

18
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:22,966
Standard normal weights make the signal explode by two orders of magnitude every layer.

19
00:02:22,966 --> 00:02:30,533
Xavier suits a linear layer, but a ReLU zeroes half its outputs, so the signal halves each layer and fades.

20
00:02:30,533 --> 00:02:38,233
He initialization compensates: half the inputs are alive, so use variance two over d. The signal stays level.

21
00:02:38,233 --> 00:02:46,300
Glorot uses two over fan-in plus fan-out, balancing forward and backward passes. For equal widths it is Xavier.

22
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:55,900
Biases are simple: start at zero, use a small constant such as 0.01, or treat the bias as one more weight.

